        ":question/id": "cf5f1258-fe1b-5b76-b089-dbb4191effcc",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-65448",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/10/10912ded2166eb7ae28ee81c2936f008bf14eb288ec95969b822dd138be509d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\pi -(-\\pi )=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1.\n$$"
      },
