        ":question/id": "d8e5a761-571d-5609-89c6-2c78742817c7",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-141974",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/2b/2b8bbc9ae6b8bf8c441c8e2b534fb159f8180833e1891baa094f988d6a362117.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $D-B?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{6\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ units up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $D-B=1-(\\frac{2}{3})=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})+1.\n$$"
      },
