        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-117796",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/92/9278723975d70fec73e313d0e164908ddd339231cb74f75b07c2edce14680111.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-(-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{1}{2}$ unit up. Hence, $D=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"
